Silent no-op for unsupported frameworks. When a user or agent runs `storybook ai init` on a Vue, Angular, Svelte, or any non-`@storybook/react-vite` project, the command exits without any output whatsoever — no error, no explanation. An agent running this command will have no way to know why nothing happened. At minimum, emit a message before returning:
```ts
if (projectInfo.framework !== '@storybook/react-vite') {
  logger.warn(`storybook ai init currently only supports @storybook/react-vite (detected: ${projectInfo.framework}). Skipping.`);
  return;
}
```
If this restriction is intentional for an MVP, it should also be reflected in the command description string.

This log line runs unconditionally for every `create-storybook` invocation. Any user who is not running inside an agent will see `Detecting agent presence: undefined` printed to their console — a confusing and unwanted debug artifact. Either remove it entirely or guard it:
```ts
const agent = detectAgent();
if (agent.name) {
  logger.log(`Running via AI agent: ${agent.name}. Enabling non-interactive defaults.`);
}
```

`detectAgent()` is invoked a second time here (and a third time at module level for `isAgent`). Each call re-reads `process.env` and iterates the checks. Cache the result once:
```ts
const detectedAgent = detectAgent();
if (detectedAgent.name) {
  options.agent = true;
  logger.log(`This command is running via an AI agent: ${detectedAgent.name}. Enabling non-interactive defaults.`);
}
```
